ged insurance group will spin  up the vms and control how they're  created  the wonderful thing about migs is  because that managed instance group is  controlling the life cycle  it can do health checks on the vms and  kill vms that aren't working correctly  and then restart them again auto scaling  events  if you have auto scaling turn on it will  start and stop vms depending on the load  so what we're doing and sven will go  into a lot more detail on this  is we're adding some features to the the  instance template  so that when the managed instance group  creates vms  it adds the mesh software to them and  allows them to join the mesh  so sven is going to go into more detail  now so i'm going to  try to give a brief overview of the  architecture of the system and i'm going  to use an example  that we're actually going to be using in  a demo later so here we have a gke  cluster  that's running anthro service mesh 1.6  and it has the online boutique sample  application  installed in it but we actually took out  the product catalog service and we moved  that over into a mig  instead of being running in a kubernetes  pod  and i want to talk a little bit about  kind of how antho service mesh  works and the services it relies on  because i think it's important to  understand just  how many different services are involved  in making the mesh actually work the way  you want to  so the first thing that happens when the  mig starts up is that it starts up this  agent that's running in the  in the mig in the vm and that agent  connects over to the dns server running  in kubernetes  qdns and acts as a proxy for that dns  server  and this is used to give service  discovery to the vm just like you get  service discovery and pods  so the same type of mesh services will  be resolved to  ip addresses in that mesh the second  service it talks to is the mesh ca  which is used to mint certificates for  the vm and the way that the this works  in practice  is that the agent is actually acting  kind of as a proxy  so it runs a secret discovery service  that the proxy can connect to  and it proxies that over to the mesh ca  and it gets the ca the ca certificates  from there  so the agent then starts up this onboard  proxy that's running inside the vm  and the proxy is configured to talk to a  control plane component  in here in this picture it's istiod  running in the cluster  and it's also configured to send  telemetry over to stackdriver  so this sends all of the logs metrics  traces  and actually also edges which we use for  our topology graph over the spectrum  and it of course connects to the agent  for those certificates  so we can get mutual tls bootstrapped  the application itself is then proxied  via that envoy proxy and that can be  done one of two ways it's either using  iptables  which is a transparent proxy mechanism  or it can actually also be run in white  box mode where the application knows  about the proxy  and sort of manually interacts with it  um asm  is also relying on the kubernetes api  server for configuration storage that's  actually not pictured here  but that's also another key component of  the system you need a place to store  your configuration things like service  definitions  authorization policies the routing  information basically all the  configuration for the features  that anthro service mesh offers is  provided in that configuration store  okay so let's walk through how we  actually add this mig to the mesh  and we're going to look at the four  different steps here so step one is  configuring the vm's identity  step two is starting up the mig and  connecting it to the services it needs  and then steps three and four are about  registering that vm  as an endpoint in this in the mesh and  selecting that endpoint  or all the endpoints that are relevant  into relevant services  so let's walk through one by one  so the first thing we're going to look  at is the identity space and this gets a  little bit complicated  because on gcp the vms are actually  running as gcp service accounts  not as kubernetes service accounts and  these have a different form so a gcp  service account  there's really two different main ways  that you'd run a vm  you can either use the default service  account for the vm which is just  project number dash compute or you can  use a named service account that you  created  which is service account name at project  and these  would be the identity that the vm starts  up with and is available through the  metadata server built into gce  but in the anthro service mesh we want  to use mesh identities so mesh  identities  are basically um three parts they are a  trust domain  or a workload pool which identifies the  source of the identities  and then they're a namespace and a  service account within that namespace  and so we need to map between these two  and asm for gce  provides a default mapping that will map  those  service accounts from gcp over into  service accounts in anthos we are also  working on making this  configurable so you can map into the  namespaces that you want to rather than  using the default  thing if that doesn't work for you  okay so now we need to actually get that  instance group  started and verify that everything is  actually working  so we do this using the mechanisms that  chris was just talking about earlier  we use an instance template that we  configure with the service proxy  we give it the configuration it needs to  know what it needs to use  and then we connect it to the control  plane components that i was just talking  about  once it's running we can verify that it  works correctly we can check that it's  connected to the control plane and is  getting configuration  we can verify it has a certificate and  rotation is set up properly  and we can make sure that telemetry is  going over to stackdriver and even go  and actually look at that telemetry if  we want to  in stackdriver to make sure it looks  like what we expect  so we have this running but we aren't  able to actually call the vm yet  so we can't route traffic to it yet so  to get traffic to the vm  we need to register it and in anthro  service mesh we do this  through a decoupled mechanism that's  very similar to how pods and services  work in kubernetes  the equivalent in anthro service mesh  for vms is workload entry and service  entry  and a workload entry is basically a  named endpoint in a namespace  it gives us a way to attach labels and  attach a service account to an endpoint  and then that thing can be selected by  the next thing we're going to talk about  which is the service entry  so a service entry basically lets us  create named mappings to endpoints in  the same way that you do with kubernetes  services  except that it can also select workload  entries so just like a kubernetes  service it has  a port or set of ports actually  it has a workload selector that says  what endpoints are selected  and in asm we also let you specify the  host  as a generic opaque string it doesn't  have to just be a kubernetes service we  can actually use other types too  but for using the same service as a  kubernetes one you can just specify the  host  like we did here okay so that's how  this all works and the architecture  let's look at a demo next so in the demo  we're actually going to set up  exactly the architecture i just talked  about  and we're going to show off the three  pillars of anthro service mesh  the observability features the uh  agility um networking and sort of  traffic management features and the  security features  so we'll look at each of those in turn  in this demo  so let's see our demo of vm's running  with antho surface mesh  so in this demo we're going to show the  kind of three main pillars of anthro  surface mesh the  observability features here in the form  of the dashboards  the traffic management capabilities  which we're going to show a little bit  of canarying  of the new version and the security  features  and here we're going to show  authorization policies and this just  gives you a little flavor of kind of  what you can do with  antho service mesh for vms it's not  meant to be a  complete demo of all the good things you  get  so let's get into it  so the first thing we're going to do is  look at what we have created so far  so we have a cluster here a gke cluster  running at the service mesh  this cluster also has the online  boutique sample application installed  except we don't have the product service  so we're also running  a managed instance group with two vms in  it  and they are running about the product  catalog service  for the online boutique instead of  finding them in the cluster so this can  show you  how you can run vms combined with your  kubernetes services  but right now we don't have these things  hooked up together so right now this is  failing because  the mesh doesn't know about those vms so  the first thing we're do is actually  show  that registration step so we're going to  create  a workload entry and a service entry for  the vm so let's look at these  so this is a workload entry for the  product catalog service b1 of it  running on an ip address so let's go  ahead and create it  and let's also look at the service entry  for this and this service entry  basically says the product catalog  service  is all of the workload entries with the  label selector below where the  app is product catalog service so this  will select everything and it's not  actually using the version we'll see  what version  is used for later and this is basically  the same as a kubernetes service except  it can select workload entries as well  as  kubernetes pods so let's go ahead and  create that  and now that we've created that our  online boutique should  be working again and there it is  it's working so once we have this up and  running the first thing we get is all  the telemetry features  so the telemetry because there's load  going through this service  all that telemetry is going to  stackdriver and we can actually see it  in the ether service mesh dashboard so  here's the dashboard for the product  catalog service we can see the requests  per second  error rates latency cpu usage things  like that  you can also use all the other great  features of antho service mesh  dashboards the  health ability to create slos look at  detailed metrics  look at connected service let's do more  diagnostic dives that kind of stuff  so all the same features you expect but  now available for a service running nfpm  okay so the next step is let's do some  traffic management so we want to roll  out  v2 of our service and the only  difference in  b1 and v2 is we're going to have a  slightly different product catalog  catalog in this case we're going to have  different product description and a  different price for this typewriter  so let's go ahead and do that so the way  we do this is we're going to create  another workload entry  and here we use b2 as the version  instead of u1  and otherwise it's the same and we don't  have to actually change  the service entry we just add a new  workload entry which is basically adding  a new endpoint  so let's create that and then we also  need to um create a  destination rule and a virtual service  so let's look at our destination rule  which actually we're already running  so this decimation rule just says that  there's two subsets to the product  cathode service there's a b1 and a v2  and those are selected based on b1 and  v2 labels so very simple  um and let's do a a traffic split so  let's split traffic  50 50 between these now normally you'd  want to do like one percent or 0.1  or something very very small when you're  carrying a new version but here we just  want to show that it works we're  going to go straight to 30  and now if we go back to our typewriter  if we refresh we should be able to see  yep there it is a different version  so the product description changed the  price change it went up a lot  new version works with their  temperatures and if you keep refreshing  you can see sometimes it'll go to b1  sometimes go to v2 it just basically is  flipping a coin and deciding which one  to do  so let's say we're happy with that and  we decide yep let's actually go  full on b2  so we can do that we have a virtual  service that says yeah send it all the  subset v2  and let's apply that  and now if we go to our batik our  typewriter should always be on youtube  and it is  okay so that's a really basic look at  some of the uh network capabilities  now let's look at our final final area  here security  and so we already have mtls set up right  so  these services are all talking to each  other over mutual tls  they each have an identity they have  certificates they're all set up but  we're not actually preventing any access  as long as something has a certificate  it gets through and so we want to do is  be able to say you know only the  services we expect to be calling are  allowed to  so what we use is asm's  authorization policy to do this and  first what we're going to do is show an  authorization policy that just locks it  down completely so that's  a good first step although he clearly  wouldn't want to do this in production  directly  but we can deny all traffic to this  product catalog service  and so all we have to do is create an  authorization policy  it has a selector that matches that  product catalog service  but it has new rules so it doesn't allow  any traffic it just denies it all so  let's go ahead and do that  and once we deny everything if we go  look at our boutique  it gives an access denied error which is  what we'd expect  okay so what about actually allowing the  traffic that we do want well so here we  have a different rule  this is again very simple we have a rule  that just says  if the principal that is calling is the  default service account in the shop  namespace  let it through and block everything else  so let's go ahead and apply that one  and now if we go look at our shop  it works again  and so that is basically what security  looks like  for antho service mesh with vms um and  so between that and the  traffic management that you get from  anthro service mesh and the great  observability features you can see  just how useful this can be for using  virtual machines without the service  mesh thanks thanks spam that was a great  demo  but i have a couple questions i've  received from customers  the first one is what about a single vm  how do i handle that  well so the great thing about migs aside  from all the great features they offer  is that actually they're free  so you can just take your vm and you can  extract an image from it which gsp makes  very easy to do  and you can run that as a single vm  in a single managed instance group and  again that's free there's no extra  charge for that  and it's very easy to do and then you  can get all the great features of megs  including the ability to run a managed  okay um and this is all great  on gce but i have anthos on-prem  and i want to integrate my on-prem vms  how do i do that  so we are actively working on making  that  both supported and very very easy it  won't be quite as easy as we've managed  to make it on gce because we don't have  the equivalent of managed instance  groups  but we do have plans in place to release  a version of anthro service mesh with vm  support for on-prem  where you take a couple more steps than  what we've outlined here  you would be responsible for generating  the configuration that goes on the vm  and installing the hmm proxy there and  we provide  packages to do that but then once you  have that everything else will be the  same as described here you can set up  the same features  it connects to the control plane the  same way and you get all the great  um anthra service mesh uh capabilities  okay and one last question what if i  have  just vms my department or division  is it using kubernetes right now i just  have vms how do i  set up the mesh with those so that is a  excellent question so  during the the architecture overview  that i was giving earlier  i was talking a little bit about the  different services that we need to  connect to  and so three of those services were  still running in the cluster so there  was the  ist2d control plane there was the cube  dns uh  dns resolver and there was the api  server used for configuration store  and the great news is we're working on  managed versions of all of those  so the control plane the managed version  is traffic director which is already  available  through gcp's apis and we're working on  making it available through  anthro surface mesh for dns  we're actively working with the cloud  dns team to actually integrate  the kubernetes dns serving and gce  dns serving into a single system so  actually that will all be built in you  won't need to do anything extra and  again it'll be a completely managed  service  and then finally we're working on making  it a managed version of the api server  so again you don't need to run anything  you can just use the managed api server  and use vms  with all those components fully managed  by google  great okay well thank you very much  yeah great questions and this was a lot  of fun thanks very much
 

